How CI runs the unit tests (.github/workflows/node.yml)

Each test job (the `runner` list in node.yml) runs `plan.js --runner=N` to
get its share of the spec files, then `run-lanes.sh`, which runs that share
as TEST_LANES lanes in parallel, each with its own Redis, MinIO, network and
copy of the working tree. A lane is a sequence of test runner processes
(`node scripts/tests <suite> --files-from=...`), one per suite it was given.

plan.js balances the lanes with timings.json: seconds per spec file, plus
per-suite entries (keyed as written in TEST_SUITES) as a fallback for files
it hasn't timed. Every test job computes the plan independently, so it only
depends on files in the checkout - never anything fetched at run time.

Refreshing timings.json, when a run's lanes finish far apart: every node run
uploads the measured seconds per spec file as the `test-timings` artifact.

  gh run download <run id> -n test-timings -D /tmp/test-timings
  jq -S -s '.[0] * .[1]' scripts/tests/ci/timings.json /tmp/test-timings/timings.json > /tmp/t.json
  mv /tmp/t.json scripts/tests/ci/timings.json

Times measured with two lanes per 4-vCPU runner include some contention, which
is fine: the plan only needs them in proportion.

To reproduce one CI process locally, take the suite and file list from the
job log and run them with `npm test <suite> -- --files-from=<file>`, or just
run the whole suite with `npm test <suite>`.
